1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,226
In the Panama Canal, why do ships

2
00:00:02,226 --> 00:00:04,587
go east to reach the Pacific Ocean?

3
00:00:04,937 --> 00:00:07,365
You'd think the Atlantic is east and

4
00:00:07,365 --> 00:00:09,659
the Pacific is west, so going from

5
00:00:09,659 --> 00:00:12,087
one to the other means heading west.

6
00:00:12,087 --> 00:00:14,111
But in Panama, the map plays a

7
00:00:14,111 --> 00:00:14,515
trick.

8
00:00:14,865 --> 00:00:17,504
Panama is shaped like a sideways S,

9
00:00:17,504 --> 00:00:19,991
lying almost east to west. So the

10
00:00:19,991 --> 00:00:22,554
canal doesn't cut east to west. It

11
00:00:22,554 --> 00:00:25,041
runs from northwest to southeast.

12
00:00:25,391 --> 00:00:27,807
That means a ship entering from the

13
00:00:27,807 --> 00:00:30,223
Atlantic side at Colón comes out on

14
00:00:30,223 --> 00:00:32,984
the Pacific side near Panama City, about

15
00:00:32,984 --> 00:00:35,055
forty kilometers farther east.

16
00:00:35,405 --> 00:00:37,937
And the canal doesn't just cut through

17
00:00:37,937 --> 00:00:40,136
land. It goes over it. Locks lift

18
00:00:40,136 --> 00:00:42,469
ships about twenty six meters up to

19
00:00:42,469 --> 00:00:45,001
Gatun Lake, a giant artificial lake in

20
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:46,733
the middle of the country.

21
00:00:47,083 --> 00:00:49,964
Each ship crossing uses millions of liters

22
00:00:49,964 --> 00:00:52,365
of fresh water from the lake, which

23
00:00:52,365 --> 00:00:54,355
flows out to the sea. In very

24
00:00:54,355 --> 00:00:56,481
dry years, the canal has had to

25
00:00:56,481 --> 00:00:58,539
limit how many ships can pass.

26
00:00:58,889 --> 00:01:01,532
Building the canal was brutal. A French

27
00:01:01,532 --> 00:01:04,582
attempt in the eighteen eighties failed after

28
00:01:04,582 --> 00:01:07,563
thousands of workers died from diseases like

29
00:01:07,563 --> 00:01:09,257
malaria and yellow fever.

30
00:01:09,607 --> 00:01:12,073
The United States took over in 1904

31
00:01:12,073 --> 00:01:14,751
and finished it ten years later, after

32
00:01:14,751 --> 00:01:18,485
doctors learned that mosquitoes spread those diseases

33
00:01:18,485 --> 00:01:19,613
and fought them.

34
00:01:19,963 --> 00:01:23,061
In 1999 the United States handed the

35
00:01:23,061 --> 00:01:26,159
canal over to Panama. Today it's run

36
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:29,515
by Panama and earns billions of dollars

37
00:01:29,515 --> 00:01:30,117
a year.

38
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:33,383
In 2016, new bigger locks opened, so

39
00:01:33,383 --> 00:01:36,785
even giant container ships can now squeeze

40
00:01:36,785 --> 00:01:39,539
through, with only a few meters to

41
00:01:39,539 --> 00:01:40,025
spare.

42
00:01:40,375 --> 00:01:42,762
The canal opened in 1914, and it

43
00:01:42,762 --> 00:01:45,299
saves ships a trip of thousands of

44
00:01:45,299 --> 00:01:48,507
kilometers around the tip of South America.

45
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:50,820
Did you know the Pacific end is

46
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:53,282
east of the Atlantic end? Tell me

47
00:01:53,282 --> 00:01:54,476
in the comments.

